    {
      "executionState": "enabled",
      "id": "note",
      "kind": "edit.markdownNote",
      "parameters": {
        "text": "# Abyssal Treasure · Submarine Expedition\nA three-level first-person underwater treasure hunt. Collect five golden coins in each level. The fifth opens the cyan exit beacon at the home buoy; return there to advance. Clear level three to win. The cockpit shows level, score, coins, depth, battery, oxygen, objective, and sonar. Space retries a failed level or starts a new campaign after victory.\n\n**Deep dives and oxygen:** The submarine starts just above the first map’s 96-metre shelf. Its winding trench is ten times wider than before and descends past 340 metres; the five caches step down from about 107 to 338 metres. The other maps keep their shallower routes. First-map steering allows steep dives and ascents. Oxygen lasts 600 seconds underwater at the default setting and refills in about four seconds near the waterline. Point the Trackpad upward to pitch up. The warning uses your depth and estimated ascent time; allow plenty of time to climb from the trench floor. Battery remains separate and does not refill at the surface.\n\n**Water and lighting:** Each map has a distinct turquoise, blue, or dark-navy water palette. The first-map water fades toward nearly black throughout the deep descent, and scene exposure falls with camera depth while the cockpit stays readable. Red sunlight fades fastest. Sand, reef, corroded metal, shells, gold and living creatures use material-specific roughness and metalness, with GGX reflections, varied surface normals, and depth-dependent diffuse light. Two side-mounted searchlights project soft conical beams in every map, lighting surfaces out to about 50 metres. The narrow beams aim outward to leave the straight-ahead view clear, and scatter through the water only up to visible objects; a little local spill reveals each lamp housing. A subtle, depth-dependent water shimmer affects the scene while cockpit text stays sharp.\n\n**Living seafloor:** Five generated materials cover sand, reef, wreckage and green and red algae. A saved-seed plant field places kelp, eelgrass, sea lettuce and red algae according to depth. The Metal world adds level-varying fan coral, anemones, shell beds, urchins, crabs, gliding turtles, bubbling vents, amphorae and salvage markers, beside fish, rays, jellyfish and the sunken vessel. Plant shapes are ray-traced, sway in the current and do not block the submarine. Life and objects extend along the deeper routes.\n\n**Generated materials:** Five fixed-seed FLUX.2 Klein 4B Distilled jobs make square seabed, reef, corroded-hull, green-algae and red-algae textures. Asset Cache retains them across Runs until inputs change or Clear is pressed. The game starts after the materials are ready; later frames reuse them. The model stays link-only. Relink all five nodes if the compatible 4B Distilled export is unavailable.\n\n**Run:** Turn off Save generated images to disk, run the workflow, and open the GPU Program full-screen preview. The submarine moves forward automatically. Drag within the preview or Trackpad: left/right steers, up/down pitches; centre flies straight. Press Space or Action to ping sonar. The radar briefly points to the nearest uncollected coin; after all five, it stays on the exit buoy. Fly within about three metres of a coin to collect it. Return to the glowing buoy near the surface after all five; the next level starts after LEVEL CLEAR.\n\n**Settings:** Edit Expedition settings JSON, then restart Run. `cruiseSpeed` is 2–10 m/s, `batterySeconds` is 90–900, `oxygenSeconds` is 90–1200, `waterVisibility` is 0.5–2 (higher means denser fog), `steeringSensitivity` is 0.4–2, and `plantDensity` is 0–2 (zero hides the generated plant field). Editing settings or the GPU Program seed restarts the GPU simulation and regenerates the same level-specific plant layout for that seed. The 1440 × 900 ray-traced frame is demanding, especially with lights; lower Width and Height if preview cadence suffers."
      },
      "resources": {},
      "title": "How to play"
    },
